  "modern": "The coining engine or mill is for handy (fig. z.), that a single man may fling twenty thousand planchets in one day; gold, silver, and copper planchets, are all of them coined with a mill, to which the coining squares (fig. z.), commonly called dyes, are fallen; that of the face under, in a square box garnified with male and female frews, to fix and keep it ready; and the other above, in a little box garnished with the same frews, to fasten the coining square. The planchet is laid flat on the square of the effigy, which is dormant; and they immediately pull the bar of the mill by its cords, which causes the frew set within it to turn. This enters into the female crew, which is in the body of the mill, and turns with too much strength, that by pulling the upper square upon that of the effigy, the planchet, violently pressed between both squares, receives the impression of both at one pull, and in the twinkling of an eye."
